const title = 'FAQ | fmlaviolationcalc.com';
const description = 'Frequently asked questions about FMLA violations: eligibility, interference vs. retaliation, liquidated damages, and filing deadlines.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Frequently Asked Questions</h1>
  <h2>Who is eligible for FMLA leave?</h2>
  <p>Employees who: (1) work for a covered employer (50+ employees within 75 miles); (2) have worked for the employer for at least 12 months; and (3) worked at least 1,250 hours in the preceding 12 months. Federal, state, and local government employees are also covered.</p>
  <h2>What qualifies as a serious health condition under the FMLA?</h2>
  <p>Any illness, injury, impairment, or physical or mental condition that involves inpatient care or continuing treatment by a health care provider. This includes chronic conditions (asthma, diabetes, depression) that require periodic treatment, as well as pregnancies, surgeries, and serious mental health conditions.</p>
  <h2>What is the difference between interference and retaliation?</h2>
  <p>Interference: the employer actively prevents an employee from taking FMLA leave (refusing the request, miscounting leave, failing to provide required notices). Retaliation: the employer takes adverse action against an employee because they took or requested FMLA leave. Both are actionable; retaliation claims often produce higher damages because they involve employment consequences.</p>
  <h2>When are liquidated damages not doubled?</h2>
  <p>The employer can defeat liquidated damages by proving: (1) the violation was in good faith; and (2) the employer had reasonable grounds to believe the act did not violate the FMLA. Both elements must be proven. Courts are skeptical of this defense when the employer had HR professionals who should have known the law.</p>
  <h2>What is the filing deadline for FMLA claims?</h2>
  <p>Two years from the date of the violation (or three years for willful violations). Unlike Title VII and the ADEA, FMLA claims do not require filing with the EEOC first &mdash; you can sue directly in federal court. Some employees also have a private cause of action through the Department of Labor.</p>
  <h2>Does the FMLA cover intermittent leave?</h2>
  <p>Yes. Intermittent FMLA leave (taking leave in separate blocks or reducing work hours) is permitted for chronic conditions, pregnancy-related conditions, and qualifying family situations. Employers cannot count intermittent FMLA leave against attendance records or use it as a basis for adverse action.</p>
</BaseLayout>
